### disc #18 IPv6 "black-hole" — CLOSED 2026-07-19, root cause was ONE bug: a MAC cloned onto the
### VLAN netdev (wan.100) only never reaches UPDMEM, so hw-offloaded IPv6 egresses with the stale
### base MAC and a MAC-bound ISP drops it (IPv4 carries its MAC inline -> unaffected). 971 below
### is the fix. The old 964/965 workarounds (decline IPv6+VLAN offload -> software) were RETIRED
### AND DELETED 2026-07-19: the "VLAN-uplink hardware black-hole" they guarded against never
### existed (the VLAN correlation = tagged-WAN ISPs are the ones whose users clone MACs), and
### 965's apparent field validation was the SW fallback masking the bug by using the correct
### netdev MAC. Recover the files from git history if ever needed.

### 971 = the disc #18 fix. HARDWARE-VALIDATED 2026-07-18/19 by BOTH original reporters on clean
### images (tompointexe: mechanism caught in the 973 debug log, then full-rate clean image;
### arnaud: the original consistent-stall setup, IPv6 up+down HW_OFFLOAD at line rate).
### Applies to pristine (must stay numbered BELOW the debug patches so a release build of 971
### alone still applies). Known limitations, disclose when upstreaming: (a) one UPDMEM slot per
### port (4-bit ADDR indexed by port->id), so routed IPv6 egressing one port with two different
### source MACs (e.g. untagged wan + cloned wan.100 at once) will fight over the slot; (b)
### unlocked write -- airoha_set_macaddr() also writes UPDMEM via a two-step DATA/CTRL handshake,
### and flow setup runs in a workqueue, not under rtnl, so a concurrent MAC change could
### interleave. Upstreaming deliberately deferred (soak first).
