### 974 = ENABLED (v3, 2026-07-21): the multi-slot fix for 971's known limitation (two source
### MACs on one port fighting over the single UPDMEM slot). Allocates a slot per distinct
### egress MAC (spinlocked — also closes 971's unlocked-write caveat), declines offload
### -EOPNOTSUPP when the pool is exhausted, logs every allocation/release. Applies ON TOP of 971.
### v1 (sticky slots 5..14) HW-validated on Gilly's test AP 2026-07-20/21: slots real, per-client
### MACs allocate + reconnect reuses its slot — but slots were NEVER FREED, so roaming/randomized
### client MACs march toward exhaustion (7/10 used in ~12h on a 3-device AP).
### v2 (2026-07-21) supersedes it with the planned refinement:
###  - REFCOUNTED slots: ref taken at flow prepare, dropped when the flow entry dies (destroy or
###    error unwind in flow_offload_replace, always after the hw FOE entry is invalidated).
###  - POOL EXTENDED to slots not owned by a registered GDM port: scan 5..14 first, then 4..0
###    (W1700K registers gdm1/2/4 -> +slot 3 and +slot 0 = 12 poolable). Port slots excluded by
###    id (not used-bit) so an unopened port can't have its slot stolen. 0xf marker untouched.
###  - v3 RELEASE-TO-CACHE (v2 soak on Gilly's AP proved refcounts balance perfectly BUT 10s
###    periodic client flows cycled alloc/release ~17k log lines/day): last ref now leaves the
###    MAC programmed as a cache entry; same MAC returning = silent revive (no reg write, no
###    log); new MAC prefers never-used slots, else evicts LRU cached slot ("slot N reassigned"
###    line). Steady-state log = SILENT; exhaustion (-EOPNOTSUPP + warn) only when >12 MACs have
###    LIVE flows at once. Live-referenced slots still can never be rewritten.
### COMPILE-VALIDATED 2026-07-21 (-Werror; allocated+reassigned+exhaustion strings in .o,
